screencast: round the frame limiter to the nearest interval boundary

Mutter (49, 50 and 51.1) drops a ScreenCast monitor-stream frame when it arrives less than one stream interval after the last
recorded one, and the stream interval is the display refresh rate. Frame timestamps are the compositor's EXPECTED presentation
times, which jitter by microseconds, so for a stream running at the refresh rate about every other frame looked "too early"
and was pushed to the next vblank: measured on the lab laptops (Iris Xe, GNOME 49.9 and 50.6, PipeWire 1.6.9) the stream
delivered exactly 30 fps at 59.9x Hz modes and ~40 fps at 60.026 Hz. Remote-desktop capture (our helper, gnome-remote-desktop)
is capped well below the display refresh because of it.

Backport of upstream mutter "stream-source: Round to nearest interval boundary in is_it_too_early" (gnome-51 branch and main,
Oct 2026, not in 51.1 or any 50.x yet): a frame is only too early when it is closer to the last recorded frame than to the next
possible one, and the "last frame" timestamp advances by whole intervals, so a stream asked for less than the refresh rate keeps
its nominal rate. Applies to mutter 49.7 .. 50.6; drop this patch when mutter >= 51.2 is the packaged version.
Measured with it on gringotts (GNOME 49.9, mutter 49.7, 1920x1080@59.934): H.264 motion path 59.6 fps (was 30.1).

--- a/src/backends/meta-screen-cast-stream-src.c
+++ b/src/backends/meta-screen-cast-stream-src.c
@@ -1281,6 +1281,7 @@
     META_SCREEN_CAST_STREAM_SRC_GET_CLASS (src);
   MetaScreenCastRecordResult record_result =
     META_SCREEN_CAST_RECORD_RESULT_RECORDED_NOTHING;
+  int64_t next_last_frame_timestamp_us = frame_timestamp_us;
 
   COGL_TRACE_BEGIN_SCOPED (MaybeRecordFrame,
                            "Meta::ScreenCastStreamSrc::maybe_record_frame_with_timestamp()");
@@ -1316,23 +1317,34 @@
         ((G_USEC_PER_SEC * ((int64_t) priv->video_format.max_framerate.denom)) /
          ((int64_t) priv->video_format.max_framerate.num));
 
+      /* Round to the nearest interval boundary (upstream mutter, "stream-source: Round to nearest interval boundary in
+       * is_it_too_early", gnome-51 branch after 51.1): a frame is only too early when it is closer to the last recorded
+       * frame than to the next possible one. The plain `delta < interval` test failed on presentation timestamp jitter
+       * when the stream frame rate equals the refresh rate and capped the stream at 30-40 fps. */
       time_since_last_frame_us = frame_timestamp_us - priv->last_frame_timestamp_us;
-      if (time_since_last_frame_us < min_interval_us)
+      if (min_interval_us > 0 &&
+          time_since_last_frame_us + min_interval_us / 2 < min_interval_us)
         {
           meta_topic (META_DEBUG_SCREEN_CAST,
-                      "Skipped recording frame on stream %u, too early",
+                      "Skipped recording frame on stream %u, too early (nearest interval boundary)",
                       priv->node_id);
           meta_screen_cast_stream_src_queue_follow_up (src, flags);
           meta_screen_cast_stream_src_accumulate_damage (src, flags, redraw_clip);
           return record_result;
         }
+
+      next_last_frame_timestamp_us =
+        MAX (priv->last_frame_timestamp_us + min_interval_us, frame_timestamp_us);
     }
 
-  return meta_screen_cast_stream_src_record_frame_with_timestamp (src,
-                                                                  flags,
-                                                                  paint_phase,
-                                                                  redraw_clip,
-                                                                  frame_timestamp_us);
+  record_result =
+    meta_screen_cast_stream_src_record_frame_with_timestamp (src,
+                                                             flags,
+                                                             paint_phase,
+                                                             redraw_clip,
+                                                             frame_timestamp_us);
+  priv->last_frame_timestamp_us = next_last_frame_timestamp_us;
+  return record_result;
 }
 
 gboolean
